You all know me, I'm always questing for memory safety approaches, looking for things to add to [the memory safety grimoire](/grimoire/grimoire).


That post was a year and a half ago, and there has been *so many new memory safety approaches* since then! My friend Nick Smith invented the [Group Borrowing](https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing) approach, Jake Fecher's [Ante](https://antelang.org) has been making waves with its [stable, mutable references](https://antelang.org/blog/stable_mutable_refs/), I made an approach called [Shared-Yet-Owned](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Q5G4qk2YO-owzqZ_-COP-rPeIxNO55VE3N3d0ulPLlk/edit?tab=t.0), Carbon's been exploring memory safety effects inspired by Nick's approach, Mojo's been experimenting with a new "origins" system, and a lot of smaller languages experimenting with new aspects.


I'll be writing about all of these in the next few months. [# Subscribe to the [RSS feed](https://verdagon.dev/rss.xml), join [r/memorysafety](https://reddit.com/r/memorysafety), or join the [discord](https://discord.gg/SNB8yGH)!]


But one article at a time! And in this one, I'm excited to _finally_ bring you some details on Mojo's work-in-progress memory safety approach. [# As far as I know, there are no explanations out there about Mojo's memory safety, so it'll be fun being the canonical reference for a while.]


! I didn't design any of this. This was designed by Chris Lattner, Nick Smith, and the Mojo team before I joined. [# TODO: doublecheck]


# "Do something crazy, then make it work."


A tried-and-true tactic for designing a memory safety approach is to:

 * Find something that _kinda maybe sort of_ could be used to help with memory safety.
 * Abandon all reason and go _all in_ on it. See how far you can take it.
 * Add other mechanisms to complement it and fill any holes.


Some examples:

 * Rust chose [shared-xor-mutable](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/book/ch04-02-references-and-borrowing.html), and then added `unsafe`, `Cell`, `RefCell`, etc. to make it work.
 * Nick's [Group Borrowing](https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing) chose mutable aliasing, and then added constraints like groups, isolation, etc. to make it work. [# TODO: doublecheck]
 * Ante went all in on [shape-stable types](https://antelang.org/blog/stable_mutable_refs/), and then used shared mutable references (`!shared`) to add more mutability in. [# TODO: doublecheck]
 * Vale originally chose [constraint references](https://verdagon.dev/blog/raii-next-steps), and then added weak references to address their main drawback.
 * Vale's later design chose [generational references](https://verdagon.dev/blog/generational-references), and then added region borrowing to make them much faster.


Basically, do something crazy, then make it work.


# What's Mojo's building block?

(This section is just me having fun building dramatic tension. [Skip ahead!](#origins))


So what's the crazy thing that Mojo is going all-in on?


A casual reading of [Mojo's Ownership](https://docs.modular.com/mojo/manual/values/ownership/) docs might lead one to think that Mojo is using normal borrow checking:

> Mojo enforces argument exclusivity for mutable references. This means that if a function receives a mutable reference to a value (such as an mut argument), it can't receive any other references to the same value—mutable or immutable. That is, a mutable reference can't have any other references that alias it.


...with a few surface-level improvements:

> The major difference between Rust and Mojo is that Mojo does not require a sigil on the caller side to pass by immutable reference.


But then you see lines in the standard library like the `->` line in [_DictEntryIter](https://github.com/modular/modular/blob/7a1e73b40ca99f150f27e0837f8565ed1f40922f/mojo/stdlib/stdlib/collections/dict.mojo#L100)'s `__next_ref__` method:


<ignore>
```mojo
    fn __next_ref__(
        mut self,
    ) -> ref [self.src[]._entries[0].value()] Self.Element:
        ...
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">&#95;&#95;next&#95;ref&#95;&#95;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">-&gt;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">src</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#95;entries</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">0</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">value</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Element</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">...</span></code></pre>


There's definitely something going on there that's not just normal borrow checking!


What is that thing those square brackets? Why is the function's signature describing so precisely what it's returning?


That, my friends, is an *origin.*


# Origins

An origin is when a reference variable *remembers what it's pointing to.*


A pointer variable in C doesn't keep track of what it's pointing to:

```c
int a = 3;
int b = 4;

int* p = &a;
// Here we know p is pointing at a, but the C compiler doesn't!

p = &b;
// Here we know p is pointing at b, but the C compiler doesn't!
```

In other words, the C compiler doesn't track a pointer variable's *origin.*



Instead of pointers like C, Mojo has references, and each reference *remembers the variable it's pointing at.*

In other words, Mojo tracks each reference's origin:


<ignore>
```mojo
var a = 3
var b = 4

# Declare a reference p that points to var a:
ref p = a
# p's type is: ref [a] Int

# Change p to point to var b:
p = b
# p's type is now: ref [b] Int
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">a</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">3</span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">b</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">4</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> Declare a reference p that points to var a:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">p</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">a</span>
<span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> p&#039;s type is: ref &#91;a] Int</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> Change p to point to var b:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">p</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">b</span>
<span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> p&#039;s type is now: ref &#91;b] Int</span></code></pre>



# Origins for Memory Safety

When a reference's type contains its origin, the compiler can use that to guarantee memory safety.


Consider this Mojo program:


<ignore>
```mojo
@fieldwise_init
struct Spaceship(Movable):
    var name: String

fn main():
    var ship = Spaceship("Serenity")
    
    ref ship_ref = ship
    # ship_ref's type: ref [ship] Spaceship

    # Destroy `ship`
    var moved_ship = ship^

    print(ship_ref.name)
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #c9d1d9">@</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">fieldwise&#95;init</span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Spaceship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Movable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">name</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">String</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">main</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">Spaceship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span><span style="color: #a5d6ff">Serenity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> ship&#95;ref&#039;s type: ref &#91;ship] Spaceship</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> Destroy `ship`</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">moved&#95;ship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span><span style="color: #ff7b72">^</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">print</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">ship&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">name</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span></code></pre>


`ship_ref`'s type isn't just `ref Spaceship`, it's `ref [ship] Spaceship`.

The remembering what it's pointing to, it's pointing to the variable `ship`.


When we compile this program, the compiler uses that information to catch a mistake:


<ignore>
```mojo
@fieldwise_init
struct Spaceship(Movable):
    var name: String

fn main():
    var ship = Spaceship("Serenity")
    
    ref ship_ref = ship
    # ship_ref's type: ref [ship] Spaceship

    # Destroy `ship`
    var moved_ship = ship^

    # error: use of uninitialized value 'ship'
    print(ship_ref.name)
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #c9d1d9">@</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">fieldwise&#95;init</span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Spaceship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Movable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">name</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">String</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">main</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">Spaceship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span><span style="color: #a5d6ff">Serenity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> ship&#95;ref&#039;s type: ref &#91;ship] Spaceship</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> Destroy `ship`</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">moved&#95;ship</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ship</span><span style="color: #ff7b72">^</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> error: use of uninitialized value &#039;ship&#039;</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">print</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">ship&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">name</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span></code></pre>


When we said `ship_ref.name`, the compiler:

 * Looked at `ship_ref`'s origin, which is `[ship]`.
 * Checked if the `ship` variable is still valid.
 * Discovered that `ship` is _not_ valid, it was previously destroyed!


This is done in a pass called CheckLifetimes (whose secrets I talked about [at LLVM](https://youtu.be/IpuvQUVB8Cg?t=808) actually!)


# Deferred Borrows [# mention research and background for deferred borrows]


This next part is going to get a bit arcane, because it's talking about Mojo's borrowing system.


The easiest way to understand Mojo's borrowing system is actually to use Rust's system as a starting point.


! If you're not familiar with how Rust works, I recommend looking at the [Group Borrowing](https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing#foundation-builds-on-cs-single-ownership) article's sections "Foundation: Builds on C++'s "Single Ownership" and "Recap of Rust Borrowing".


Take this simple Rust program, that shows how we can't have a mutable reference and a read-only reference to the same object at the same time.


```rs
struct Entity { 
    health: i32,
    mana: i32,
}
struct Game {
    player_id: usize,
    entities: Vec<Entity>
}
fn cast_heal(game: &mut Game, caster_id: usize, receiver_id: usize) {
    let caster_ref = &mut game.entities[caster_id];
    let receiver_ref = &mut game.entities[receiver_id];

    caster_ref.mana -= 10;
    receiver_ref.health += 30;
}
```


Rust rejects this code because it doesn't allow you to have multiple mutable references to the same thing, because that's sometimes unsafe.


Mojo doesn't have that error:


<ignore>
```mojo
struct Entity(Copyable, Movable):
    var health: Int
    var mana: Int

struct Game:
    var player_id: Int
    var entities: InlineArray[Entity, 2]

fn cast_heal(mut game: Game, caster_id: Int, receiver_id: Int):
    ref caster_ref = game.entities[caster_id]
    ref receiver_ref = game.entities[receiver_id]

    caster_ref.mana -= 10
    receiver_ref.health += 30

```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Entity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Copyable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Movable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">player&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">InlineArray</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Entity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">2</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">-=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">10</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">+=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">30</span>
<span></span></code></pre>


This is because in Mojo, reference variables (like `caster_ref` and `receiver_ref`) don't actually care whether they're mutable.


For example, in this line in the Rust program:

`let caster_ref = &mut game.entities[caster_id];`

`caster_ref`'s type is '&mut Entity`.


But in the Mojo line:

`ref caster_ref = game.entities[caster_id]`

`caster_ref`'s type doesn't contain the mutability, instead it's something like `ref [game.entities[*]]`. [# I think technically it's `game.entities[0]`, but you get the idea.]


A Mojo reference checks for safety when it's _used_, not when it's _created._


In other words, Mojo does its safety checks here:

```
    caster_ref.mana -= 10
    receiver_ref.health += 30
```

When it does `caster_ref.mana -= 10`, it:

 * Looks at `caster_ref`'s origin, which is `[game.entities[*]]`.
 * Checked if `game.entities[*]` is still valid.
 * Nothing might have destroyed anything in `game.entities`, so we're good!


Let's call these "easy references", they're references that _don't remember their mutability._


These are also known as deferred borrows, [# TODO: add more background and related work]


I like easy references, because (in my opinion) they more accurately model the world, and that have more potential for better error messages. [# This is one reason I liked Group Borrowing so much; my interpretation is that every reference is a easy reference, and mutation is expressed as effects.]


That said, *not all references in Mojo are easy references.* Mojo also has "read references" and "mut references" which are basically Rust's `&` and `&mut`.


Imagine we pulled the health and mana adjustments out into a `cast_heal_spell` function like this:


<ignore>
```mojo
struct Player(Copyable, Movable):
    var health: Int
    var mana: Int

struct Game:
    var player_id: Int
    var entities: List[Player]

fn cast_heal_spell(
    mut caster: Player,
    mut receiver: Player,
    amount: Int
):
    caster.mana -= amount
    receiver.health += amount

fn cast_heal(mut game: Game, caster_id: Int, receiver_id: Int):
    ref caster_ref = game.entities[caster_id]
    ref receiver_ref = game.entities[receiver_id]

    cast_heal_spell(caster_ref, receiver_ref, 10);
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Copyable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Movable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">player&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">List</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal&#95;spell</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">caster</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">receiver</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">amount</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">-=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">amount</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">+=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">amount</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal&#95;spell</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">10</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">;</span></code></pre>


If we try to compile it, this gives us errors:


<ignore>
```mojo
fn cast_heal(mut game: Game, caster_id: Int, receiver_id: Int):
    ref caster_ref = game.entities[caster_id]
    ref receiver_ref = game.entities[receiver_id]

    # error: argument of 'cast_heal_spell' call allows writing a memory location previously writable through another aliased argument
    cast_heal_spell(caster_ref, receiver_ref, 10);
    #              ^~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~
    # note: 'game.entities' value is passed through aliasing 'mut' argument
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> error: argument of &#039;cast&#95;heal&#95;spell&#039; call allows writing a memory location previously writable through another aliased argument</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">cast&#95;heal&#95;spell</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">10</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">;</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e">              ^~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #8b949e">#</span><span style="color: #8b949e"> note: &#039;game.entities&#039; value is passed through aliasing &#039;mut&#039; argument</span></code></pre>


When we try to turn an easy reference into a mut reference, Mojo invokes Rust-like borrow checking rules.

In the above example, when it gets to the `cast_heal_spell(caster_ref, receiver_ref, 10)` line, it:

 * Checks the `caster_ref` argument:
    * Looks up `caster_ref`'s origin, which is `game.entities[*]`.
    * Checks that nobody is borrowing `game.entities[*]` or anything inside it.
    * Nobody is, so continue on!
    * Marks `game.entities[*]` as "temporarily mutably borrowed".
 * Checks the `receiver_ref` argument:
    * Looks up `receiver_ref`'s origin, which is `game.entities[*]`.
    * Checks that nobody is borrowing `game.entities[*]` or anything inside it.
    * Someone is already borrowing `game.entities[*]`! *Halt* and give a compiler error!


In other words, we only get easy references' benefits until we try to put something in a read reference or a mut reference.


Mojo operates under these rules:

 * All `ref` variables are easy references.
 * All function parameters are either read references or mut references.


So in effect, Mojo *waits until the callsite* to:

 * Determine if a part of an object is being borrowed mutably or immutably.
 * Check if there's any overlap with any other arguments.


If your program was entirely in main, you'd be able to program entirely with easy references. But of course, once functions get involved, you lose these benefits, mostly.

I say "mostly" because there is one place where origin information travels across function boundaries: return ref origins!


# Return Ref Origins

I showed this snippet before:


<ignore>
```mojo
    fn __next_ref__(
        mut self
    ) -> ref [self.src[]._entries[0].value()] Self.Element:
        ...
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">&#95;&#95;next&#95;ref&#95;&#95;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">-&gt;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">src</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#95;entries</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">0</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">value</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Element</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">...</span></code></pre>


This line is going to show us how we can communicate origins across function boundaries.


It is The Most Interesting Line in the Standard Library.

<img src="https://i.imgflip.com/abbs2e.jpg"/>

(That's a [reference](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8nt94LCyqY), I couldn't help myself.)


Mojo is a work in progress and the syntax is still rough, so I'll interpret that line for us: [#aliasorigin]


<ignore>
```mojo
    fn __next_ref__(
        mut self
    ) -> ref [self._entries[*]] Self.Element:
        ...
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">&#95;&#95;next&#95;ref&#95;&#95;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        mut </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">-&gt;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#95;entries</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #ff7b72">*</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Self</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Element</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">        </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">...</span></code></pre>


That's basically saying that it's returning a reference to an `Element` type, specifically one in `self._entries`.


In other words, it's *communicating an origin across function boundaries* in its return type.


Let's make a similar function for our healing example:


<ignore>
```mojo
struct Player(Copyable, Movable):
    var health: Int
    var mana: Int

struct Game:
    var player_id: Int
    var entities: List[Player]

fn get_entity(
    ref [_] game: Game,
    entity_id: Int
) -> ref [game.entities[0]] Player:
    return game.entities[entity_id]

fn cast_heal(mut game: Game, caster_id: Int, receiver_id: Int):
    ref caster_ref = get_entity(game, caster_id)
    ref receiver_ref = get_entity(game, receiver_id)

    caster_ref.mana -= 10
    receiver_ref.health += 20
```
</ignore>


<pre style="background-color: black; padding: 16px; font-size: 80%; overflow-x: auto;"><code class="nohighlight"><span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Copyable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Movable</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">struct</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ffa657">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">player&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">var</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">List</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #ff7b72">fn</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">get&#95;entity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    ref &#91;&#95;] </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">entity&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Int</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">-&gt;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #79c0ff">0</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">Player</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">:</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">return</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entities</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&#91;</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">entity&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">]</span>
