<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">get&#95;entity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">caster&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">get&#95;entity</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">(</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">,</span><span style="color: #d2a8ff"> </span><span style="color: #d2a8ff">receiver&#95;id</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">)</span>
<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">-=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">10</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">+=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">20</span></code></pre>


This `get_entity` function isn't just returning a "reference to a Player", it's returning a "reference to a Player in game.entities".

(Disregard the 0 in `game.entities[0]`, that 0 is actually ignored. Mojo interprets this as "anything in entities".)

`cast_heal` sees that information, and knows that `caster_ref` and `receiver_ref` are both pointing into `game.entities`.




! Everything below here is speculation. [# TODO: doublecheck all of this so we can be clear whether its speculation or real.]


You might be wondering, why is that useful? What does that get us?

It means we can freely modify other parts of the `game`.

Take this example, where we modify a `last_action_message` in game:



<ignore>
```mojo
struct Player(Copyable, Movable):
    var health: Int
    var mana: Int

struct Game:
    var player_id: Int
    var entities: List[Player]
    var last_action_message: String

fn get_entity(
    ref [_] game: Game,
    entity_id: Int
) -> ref [game.entities[0]] Player:
    return game.entities[entity_id]

fn cast_heal(mut game: Game, caster_id: Int, receiver_id: Int):
    ref caster_ref = get_entity(game, caster_id)
    ref receiver_ref = get_entity(game, receiver_id)

    game.last_action_message = "Someone cast a healing spell!"
    caster_ref.mana -= 10
    receiver_ref.health += 20
```
</ignore>


