<span></span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">game</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">last&#95;action&#95;message</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span><span style="color: #a5d6ff">Someone cast a healing spell!</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">&quot;</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">caster&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">mana</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">-=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">10</span>
<span style="color: #c9d1d9">    </span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">receiver&#95;ref</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">.</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9">health</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #ff7b72">+=</span><span style="color: #c9d1d9"> </span><span style="color: #79c0ff">20</span></code></pre>


The compiler still knows that these lines are safe:

```
    caster_ref.mana -= 10
    receiver_ref.health += 20
```

because it knows nothing has deleted any `Entity`s.


"But Evan, we modified `game`! Are you sure `caster_ref` and `receiver_ref` are still valid?"


Good question! And indeed, the conservative stance would be that if you modified _anything_ in `Game`, then you shouldn't use _any_ reference from before that modification.


But since easy references have origins, they don't need to be conservative.


Let's walk through the `can_heal` function step by step to show what I mean:


First, we have this line:

`ref caster_ref = get_entity(game, caster_id)`

`get_entity` returns a `ref [game.entities[0]] Entity` which means "any entity".

So `caster_ref`'s type is `ref [game.entities[0]] Entity`.

(Remember, the 0 is ignored, so this really means "any Entity in game.entities")


Same thing happens with `receiver_ref`.


Next, we have the line `game.last_action_message = "Someone cast a healing spell!"`.

The compiler internally notes down that we changed `game.last_action_message`.


Now we're at `caster_ref.mana -= 10`.

The compiler does its normal safety check:

 * Looks at `caster_ref`'s origin, which is `[game.entities[0]]`.
 * Checked anyone might have destroyed `game.entities[0]` since we got this reference.
 * Nothing has touched `game.entities`, so we're good!


Same thing happens with the `receiver_ref.health += 20` line, of course.


So, the big takeaway is that these last two lines are safe because nobody's modified `game.entities`, only `game.entities[0]`.


<slice>
#aliasorigin: Fun fact, we could theoretically say `-> ref [self.EntryOrigin] Self.Element` if we define a `alias EntryOrigin = self.src[]._entries[0].value()` above it.

In fact, this will help it conform to certain `Iterator`-like traits. The trait could hold an origin as an associated type.
</slice>


# The Missing Pieces [# TODO: tone down the above rust comparisons because this is all aspirational and WIP]


Like I said, the above is partially speculation [# can it not be speculation please lol] because there are some pieces missing in the implementation:

 * Mojo can't tell the difference between `game.entities` and `game.entities[0]`.
 * Mojo doesn't actually have the logic of "if you modified game.entities, invalidate game.entities[0] refs".


Mojo's current implementation does some things:

 * Prevents overlapping borrows ("I can't take a mut reference to field x.y.z because that's already being passed in as another argument")
 * Prevents using a reference to something that was moved away or destroyed ("I can't use a reference to field x.y.z because someone moved x away with an `x^`") [# TODO: explain the caret syntax]

But it doesn't yet:

 * Prevent using a reference to something that might be gone because someone _modified_ it ("I can't use a reference to a field `x.my_list[*]`" because someone modified `x.my_list`)


My impatient self wants it to be memory safe _now_ so I can actually use it for larger projects, but alas. Patience! Patience.


TODO: modular.com actually says Mojo is memory-safe, holy lord


# The approach so far, summarized

There are three kinds of references:

 * Easy references, declared via `ref x = ...` in local variables.
 * Mut references, declared via `mut x: SomeType` in function parameters.
 * Read references, declared via `read x: SomeType` in function parameters (well, that's the default, so it's really just `x: SomeType`)


An easy reference's type remembers what variable they're pointing to. For example, in `ref e = ship.engine`, `e`'s type is `ref [ship.engine] Engine`.


Mojo uses that origin to enforce memory safety *on use*. It will enforce it in a few ways:

 * Checks the origins to prevent overlapping borrows when calling other functions.
 * Checks the origins when dereferencing a reference, checking if it points to data we moved away or destroyed.
 * (Someday) Checks the origins when dereferencing a reference, checking if it points to data owned by something someone else modified.


# Where does the approach go from here?


To see where Mojo could go, let's look at its goals, challenges, and potential.


## Mojo's Memory Safety Goals


Mojo's goals (from the [vision doc](https://docs.modular.com/mojo/vision) and [roadmap](https://docs.modular.com/mojo/roadmap/)) can be summarized like this:

 * Be the fast language in existence, mainly using its [metaprogramming powers](https://verdagon.dev/blog/impossible-optimization) and its ability to compile to all sorts of accelerators like GPUs.
 * Bring that speed in a memory-safe way to Python users.


Mojo is an AI company, so in AI terms:

 * Most ML/AI research is done by researches using Python (easy, slow, safe) who then have to hand off their designs to kernel ninjas who implement it in C++ (hard, fast, unsafe) and optimize it for GPUs.
 * Mojo's goal is to be easy, fast, and safe, so that those researchers can prototype in Python and easily optimize. [# Currently that optimization is done mostly by the built-in graph compiler, but I see a future where users can also express [impossible optimizations](https://verdagon.dev/blog/impossible-optimization) and then migrate them to compiler plugins... we'll see though.]


But for the rest of us who don't think in terms of AI, I'll translate that:

 * Python has a massive ecosystem and a lot of programmers who _want to go fast._ That's why it has C extensions, why Cython exists, and why people call from Python into Rust with things like PyO3.
 * Mojo's goal is to be easy, fast, and safe, so that python programmers can prototype in Python and easily migrate to Mojo when they want more speed.


These goals affect every aspect of the language. For memory safety, these goals become:

 * Simplicity: how easy can the memory safety approach become?
 * Progressive disclosure: what's the learning curve from a Python starting point, and how easy will it be for Python users to gradually learn and migrate to? 


The current approach is maybe 20% of the way there on the first one (easy references are more Pythonic than mut references and read references, but they can't travel very far), and about 10% of the way there on the second one (Mojo has naming and syntax that is similar to Python at a surface-level).


So let's talk potential! How far might it go towards those goals?


## The Challenges


There's a lot of directions Mojo can go, and I think they revolve around these two questions:

 1. Will Mojo go all-in on shared mutability, or will fall back to a Rust-like approach?
 1. Will it be able to keep those benefits if/when it adds reference counting?


Regarding that first one, Mojo's current design seems a bit conflicted, because it has both easy references and also Rust-like read/mut references (which makes sense given its work-in-progress status).

This isn't a favorable spot to stay in, because it's actually _harder_ to understand.

Context: Understanding Rust relies on understanding the core principle of shared-xor-mutable, which they _consistently_ apply everywhere in their language. While the principle itself is unintuitive, its _consistency_ helps people learn the language.

Mojo has to explain easy references, _plus_ Rust's approach, but without the consistency that helps people understand Rust's approach today.


The second challenge is a classic problem in language design. Reference counting and borrowing mechanisms seem to _hate_ each other. No language has pulled it off well yet.

But Python users (including me!) want reference counting. Reference counting is a clearer, simpler, and more flexible memory safety method.

It's also important because it gives Python users something to translate to, as an intermediate step towards speed.


Many languages have tried this:

 * Rust has Rc<RefCell<T>> but that risks panics and doesn't solve the mutability problem, it just delays it.
 * ZZZ1 Swift ?
 * ZZZ2 D ?
 * ZZZ3 V tried this with autofree and failed
 * ZZZ4 Vale has the regions / reference counting thing, but it boots objects to the heap.


It's hard because:

 * ZZZ5 language killer
 * ZZZ6 tree structured 


But if a language can blend the two gracefully, then they've achieved something great. This is the holy grail, something I'll be writing about a lot in coming months. It's closer than we think.


## The Potential

In my biased opinion, the potential is pretty massive. [# Well, of course I would, this is the main reason I joined Mojo in the first place!] Seamlessly blending mutable aliasing and borrowing and reference counting is the holy grail of memory safety, because it would actually enable a language that's fast, easy, and safe, something no language has really achieved yet. [# Vale _technically_ achieved these goals, but it risked accidental generational reference panics a little too much for me (despite regions being such a great mitigation) to consider it the holy grail. Maybe the holy grail's criteria should be "fast, easy, safe, and predictable" or something? Dunno.]


To go from here to there, I can see a few paths. I haven't thoroughly explored these as much as I want to, but they're a promising start.


For the shared mutability aspect:

 * Adding origins to function parameters seems like a reasonable next step. There's a place in the syntax for it already (notice the `ref [_] game: Game` above), and the compiler infrastructure is mostly set up for it already, from what I can see.
 * Long term, going for something like the group borrowing design could be a good idea. [# Or as I call it, "going full Nick".]
 * If anyone sees any other way to make borrowing more intuitive, let me know!


For the reference counting aspect, there are a few approaches:

 * Do something like this [reference counting / region borrowing blend](https://github.com/verdagon/RegionsRCCellularAutomataBenchmark) I stumbled upon when working on Vale, then bring in parts of [the](https://verdagon.dev/blog/zero-cost-borrowing-regions-part-2-isolates) [advanced](https://verdagon.dev/blog/zero-cost-borrowing-regions-part-3-one-way-isolation) [regions](https://verdagon.dev/blog/zero-cost-borrowing-regions-part-4-multi-region-data) [designs](https://verdagon.dev/blog/zero-cost-borrowing-regions-part-5-region-scoped-data).
 * [Shared-Yet-Owned](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Q5G4qk2YO-owzqZ_-COP-rPeIxNO55VE3N3d0ulPLlk/edit?tab=t.0) is a promising route to explore, especially since it shows how reference counting composes well with single ownership (and linear types!). It's a bit too _out there_ to seriously consider, but a final approach could benefit from some pieces of this.
 * The best idea though, I'm keeping as a surprise for now. I think I might see a path towards an optimal blend, but it requires a very subtle and unexpected shift. If you're curious, find me on [my discord](https://discord.gg/SNB8yGH)! [# I also accept vicarious bribes in the form of donations to the [Kākāpō Recovery](https://www.doc.govt.nz/kakapo-donate) or [memory safety designers](https://github.com/sponsors/nmsmith) or [open source programming languages](https://ziglang.org/zsf/). Look at those adorable Kākāpō birds. Donate now, and don't listen to your own excuses for not donating!]


# Conclusion


Pretty promising stuff! It'll be interesting to see where it goes, and it'll be fun to try out a fully memory-safe Mojo one day.


It might be a while though. Memory safety is actually pretty low on Mojo's priority list, so don't expect Mojo to be a real memory-safe language any time soon. [# They [aren't interested in design discussions](https://docs.modular.com/mojo/roadmap) about memory safety, which is unusual for something so foundational. Alas!] But even though the team isn't working on memory safety, the building blocks and the direction are still very interesting, and it's fun to think about.


If you also think this stuff is fun to think about, feel free to ask questions in my new subreddit [r/memorysafety](https://reddit.com/r/memorysafety), and you can also find me in the [Vale discord](https://discord.gg/SNB8yGH).


Next up, I'll be posting about how the Carbon team is designing Group Borrowing into Carbon, so stay tuned and subscribe to the [RSS feed](https://verdagon.dev/rss.xml)!


Cheers,

- Evan




<ignore>
So, TL;DR: We can pass origins across function boundaries, but only in return types.

"So wait, does this mean Mojo programs don't need to look like Rust programs?"

With just the features described so far, programs will still look like Rust programs. Users will still be fighting the borrow checker in Mojo, even with return ref origins.

It's a bit inconsistent, but it's nice that they get a small ergonomic benefit intra-function and in function return types.

------


# Why this is exciting

This is another case of how Mojo is going in a very exciting direction: it's laying the groundwork for easier memory-safe high-performance code.


This is something that most of the world believes is impossible.


This is coincidentally, the exact sort of thing Vale was chasing. (explain)


This is also what Nick's [Group Borrowing](https://verdagon.dev/blog/group-borrowing#foundation-builds-on-cs-single-ownership) design is trying to achieve.


Ante is also making some exciting strides here.


Niko Matsakis, the Rust tech lead, has also written about this (link to View Types post).


All these languages are chasing the same thing: easier memory-safe high-performance code. And they're all heading in roughly the same direction: removing complexity.


------




The first thing to note is that Mojo's memory safety has no run-time overhead. [# Well, it's complicated; every memory-safe language has some run-time overhead, see [Chasing the Myth of Zero-Overhead Memory Safety](https://verdagon.dev/blog/myth-zero-overhead-memory-safety).]


------


This next part is going to get a bit arcane, because it's talking about Mojo's borrowing system.


However, there are two major differences between Mojo and Rust:

 * Mojo uses "origin paths" to allow mutable aliasing inside the current function.
 * In some cases, Mojo communicates origin paths between functions.

I'll explain them below. Read on!


------


This is _really nice,_ especially for those learning the language. Mutability is hard to understand, and being able to program without thinking about mutability is one of the bigger benefits of Python.


Unfortunately, this benefit doesn't last long: Mojo can only disregard mutability _inside_ a function. Once you start to call other things, it cares.


This is, in my opinion, a big step in the right direction, because it relaxes the restrictions that normal borrow checking had. As far as the Mojo compiler is concerned, these are perfectly safe operations, so why not allow them?


-----


Sometimes I talk as if memory safety approaches manifest themselves into our plane of existence, like some sort of summoned elementals. It actually takes a _long_ time to design a memory safety model, usually years.


-----



